  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1045 PM MDT Fri Aug 7 2026\n\nHOT AND DRY THROUGH THIS WEEKEND:\n\nHigh pressure will remain anchored over the Four Corners region\nthis weekend. We will see a bit more cloud cover advect into the\nregion from the west as some moisture sneaks in under the ridge,\nguided by a few shortwaves that will move around the ridge and\nacross the Northern Rockies. This cloud cover comes in a bit \nlater in the day on Saturday, so overnight lows Saturday night \nappear to be much warmer than we've seen recently due to lack \nof efficient radiational cooling in the absence of clearer \nskies. Temperatures during the afternoon will remain hot with a\ndry air mass remaining in place. Even though a bit of moisture \nwill result in more cloud cover this weekend and some elevated \nconvection over the high terrain, it will take some time for us \nto overcome this hot and dry air mass in place. So any storms \nthat do form will be more prone to gusty outflow winds and \nlightning with a very low chance of wetting rain (5 percent or \nless). Heat advisories remain in place Saturday and Sunday for \nour east-central and southeast Utah desert valleys with the \nGrand Valley in west-central Colorado only seeing Heat Advisory\nfor Saturday as Sunday is expected to be below Heat Advisory \nthresholds.\n\nMOISTURE RETURNS NEXT WEEK:\n\nA pattern change is on the horizon next week as moisture\ncontinues to increase from the west underneath the high. Storm\ncoverage as a result is expected to increase but appears like\nearly next week will favor the high terrain with better chances\nof wetting rain while the lower valleys remain mostly dry. It\nappears like deterministic and ensemble models are honing in on\nthe mid to late week timeframe for increased storm coverage and\nprecipitation potential. This is likely due to a low pressure\ntrough setting up off the west coast by mid to late week,\nallowing the high pressure ridge to shift eastward enough to\ndraw a more direct southerly tap of monsoonal moisture. The late\nweek period looks particularly interesting as the GFS is\nindicating a rather robust shortwave moving through the Four\nCorners and up through western Colorado next Thursday. With the\naddition of forcing provided by several shortwaves, this could\nresult in some overnight convection as well in the late week\nperiod. These details however are not certain and uncertainty \nstill remains with regards to the magnitude of this moisture \npush, but models continue to favor a mid to late week jump in \nQPF, PWAT and PoP.",
